"Linearization Explains Fine-Tuning in Large Language Models (https://arxiv.org/html/2602.08239v1)\nciteturn31650view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn31639view2\",\"lineno\":194}); Total lines: 810\nL176: Despite the fact that NTK analysis often considers the entire model, it is straightforward to specialize it to the fine-tuning case, which we do here for completeness. When using mean squared error, fine-tuning with the linear model is similarly equivalent to solving the kernel regression problem presented in (cite64†14 ). Let $\\mathcal{H}$ be the reproducing kernel Hilbert space (RKHS) endowed with a positive definite kernel function $\\mathbf{k}(\\cdot,\\cdot)$, i.e.,\nL177:  | $$\\mathcal{H}=\\bigg\\{f(\\cdot)=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\alpha_{i}\\mathbf{k}(\\cdot,\\mathbf{x}_{i})\\bigg\\}.$$  |\nL178: \nL179: Assuming the solution lies in or close to this Hilbert space, then as an alternative to (cite51†1 ), we solve\nL180: \nL181:  | $\\displaystyle\\;\\underset{f\\in\\mathcal{H}}{\\textrm{minimize}}\\;\\;\\frac{1}{n}\\sum_{\\left(\\mathbf{x},\\mathbf{y}\\right)\\in\\mathcal{D}_{T}}\\left\\|f\\left(\\mathbf{x}\\right)-\\mathbf{y}\\right\\|^{2}+\\sigma\\|f\\|_{\\mathcal{H}}^{2},$  |  | (14)\nL182: where $\\|\\cdot\\|_{\\mathcal{H}}$ is the norm corresponding to the inner product $\\langle\\cdot,\\cdot\\rangle_{\\mathcal{H}}$ defined on the RKHS $\\mathcal{H}$. The parameter $\\sigma>0$ regularizes the problem toward minimum-norm solutions and is qualitatively inversely related to the number of gradient descent steps in the original problem.\nL183: We thus effectively formulate the fine-tuning problem as a regularized function estimation in the RKHS, $\\mathcal{H}$, generated by the NTK, $\\mathbf{k}(\\mathbf{x},\\mathbf{x}^{\\prime})=\\nabla f_{\\bm{\\theta}_{0}}(\\mathbf{x})\\nabla f_{\\bm{\\theta}_{0}}(\\mathbf{x}^{\\prime})^{\\top}$. According to the representer theorem  [cite65†19 ], the problem (cite64†14 ) on training dataset $\\mathcal{D}_{T}=\\{\\mathbf{x}_{i},\\mathbf{y}_{i}\\}_{i=1}^{n}$ possesses the closed-form solution\nL184:  | $$f^{*}(\\cdot)=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\alpha_{i}\\mathbf{k}\\left(\\cdot,\\mathbf{x}_{i}\\right)=\\bm{\\alpha}^{\\top}\\mathbf{K}(\\cdot,\\mathbf{X}),$$  |  | (15)\nL185: \nL186: where $\\mathbf{K}(\\cdot,\\mathbf{X})=[\\mathbf{k}(\\cdot,\\mathbf{x}_{1}),\\ldots,\\mathbf{k}(\\cdot,\\mathbf{x}_{n})]\\in\\mathbb{R}^{1\\times n}$ . Substituting (cite66†15 ) in (cite64†14 ), we have\nL187:  | $\\displaystyle\\;\\underset{\\bm{\\alpha}}{\\textrm{minimize}}\\;\\;\\mathbb{E}_{(\\mathbf{x},\\mathbf{y})\\sim\\mathcal{D}_{T}}\\left\\|\\bm{\\alpha}^{\\top}\\mathbf{K}(\\cdot,\\mathbf{X})-\\mathbf{Y}\\right\\|^{2}+\\sigma\\|f\\|_{\\mathcal{H}}^{2},$  |  | (16)\nL188: \nL189: which is a convex problem with $\\bm{\\alpha}^{*}=\\left[\\mathbf{K}\\left(\\mathbf{X}_{,}\\mathbf{X}\\right)+\\sigma\\mathbf{I}\\right]^{-1}\\mathbf{Y}$ as the solution. Equivalently,\nL190:  | $\\displaystyle f^{*}(\\cdot)=\\mathbf{K}\\left(\\cdot,\\mathbf{X}\\right)\\left[\\mathbf{K}\\left(\\mathbf{X}_{,}\\mathbf{X}\\right)+\\sigma\\mathbf{I}\\right]^{-1}\\mathbf{Y},$  |  | (17)\nL191: \nL192: where $[\\mathbf{K}(\\mathbf{X},\\mathbf{X})]_{i,j}=\\mathbf{k}(\\mathbf{x}_{i},\\mathbf{x}_{j})$. For the sake of brevity, hereafter, we use $\\mathbf{K}(\\mathbf{X},\\mathbf{X})$ and $\\mathbf{K}$ interchangeably.\nL193: Under linearity, we now show through Theorem cite67†5 that the spectrum of the NTK directly affects the empirical risk, making it a tool that can be used for making fine-tuning decisions.\nL194: ###### Theorem 5.\nL195: \nL196: The empirical risk is bounded as\nL197: \nL198:  | $$\\left(\\frac{\\sigma\\left\\|\\mathbf{Y}\\right\\|}{\\sigma+\\lambda_{\\text{max}}(\\mathbf{K})}\\right)^{2}\\leq{\\mathcal{R}}(\\bm{\\theta})\\leq\\left(\\frac{\\sigma\\left\\|\\mathbf{Y}\\right\\|}{\\sigma+\\lambda_{\\text{min}}(\\mathbf{K})}\\right)^{2},$$  |  | (18)\nL199: \nL200: where $\\lambda_{\\text{min}}(\\mathbf{K})$ and $\\lambda_{\\text{max}}(\\mathbf{K})$ are the minimum and maximum eigenvalues of $\\mathbf{K}(\\mathbf{X},\\mathbf{X})$, respectively.\nL201: ###### Proof.\nL202: \nL203: See Appendix cite22†F . ∎\nL204: Theorem cite67†5 motivates us to study the regularized condition number $\\kappa(\\mathbf{K}+\\sigma\\mathbf{I})=\\frac{\\lambda_{\\text{max}}(\\mathbf{K})+\\sigma}{\\lambda_{\\text{min}}(\\mathbf{K})+\\sigma}$ as an at-initialization metric for predicting the performance of fine-tuning.\nL205: To show how this can be useful, for example, in selecting what subset of parameters to tune, we next study the randomized kernel specifically for fine-tuning, which enables us to investigate the training dynamics based on the influence of each individual trainable parameter on the bounds in Theorem cite67†5 .\nL206: ## 5 Spectral Perturbation of Layers\nL207: We have shown that (i) regularized fine-tuning is well-approximated by its fully linearized counterpart, (ii) that optimizing the linearized fine-tuning is equivalent to NTK kernel regression, and (iii) that the kernel’s spectrum is predictive of the fine-tuning performance. To put these facts to use, in this section, we study the effect of layer selection on the spectrum of the NTK, and consequently on the empirical risk of fine-tuning.\nL208: Let $\\bm{\\theta}^{l}$ be the parameters of the layer $l$ from the pretrained model and $\\bm{\\theta}=\\{\\bm{\\theta}^{1},\\ldots,\\bm{\\theta}^{L}\\}$ be the set of parameters of the $L$ layers from the pretrained model. The NTK, when the parameters in $\\bm{\\theta}$ are chosen for fine-tuning, is $[\\mathbf{K}]_{i,j}=\\sum_{l=1}^{L}\\nabla_{\\bm{\\theta}^{l}}f_{\\bm{\\theta}}\\left(\\mathbf{x}_{i}\\right)\\nabla_{\\bm{\\theta}^{l}}f_{\\bm{\\theta}}(\\mathbf{x}_{j})^{\\top}$.\nL209: The kernel induced by $\\bm{\\theta}^{l}$ is $\\mathbf{S}_{l}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{n\\times n}$, where\nL210:  | $$[\\mathbf{S}_{l}]_{i,j}=\\nabla_{\\bm{\\theta}^{l}}f_{\\bm{\\theta}}\\left(\\mathbf{x}_{i}\\right)\\nabla_{\\bm{\\theta}^{l}}f_{\\bm{\\theta}}(\\mathbf{x}_{j})^{\\top}.$$  |  | (19)\nL211: \nL212: One can infer that the NTK induced by all layers is $\\mathbf{K}=\\sum_{l=1}^{L}\\mathbf{S}_{l}$.\nL213: \nL214: The following theorems quantify the effect of adding a set of trainable parameters on the spectrum of the NTK.\nL215: ###### Theorem 6.\nL216: \nL217: Let $\\mathbf{K}$ be the NTK with respect to the set of selected fine-tuning parameters, and $\\mathbf{S}$ be the kernel with respect to the parameters of the candidate layers, to add to the fine-tuning parameters. Then\nL218: \nL219:  | $\\displaystyle(1-\\eta)\\lambda_{i}(\\mathbf{K})\\leq\\lambda_{i}(\\mathbf{K}+\\mathbf{S})\\leq(1+\\eta)\\lambda_{i}(\\mathbf{K}),$  |  | (20)\nL220: \nL221: where $\\eta=\\|\\mathbf{K}^{-1/2}\\mathbf{S}\\;\\mathbf{K}^{-1/2}\\|$.\nL222: ###### Proof.\nL223: \nL224: See Appendix cite23†G . ∎\nL225: \nL226: The spectral perturbation bounds of Theorem cite68†6 can interact with the risk bounds given by Theorem cite67†5 , to help us further understand the relative merits of parameter choices. We denote by $\\mathcal{R}(\\bm{\\theta})$ and $\\mathcal{R}(\\bm{\\theta}\\cup\\hat{\\bm{\\theta}})$, respectively, the empirical risk of the model fine-tuned with parameters $\\bm{\\theta}$ and $\\bm{\\theta}\\cup\\hat{\\bm{\\theta}}$.\nL227: ###### Theorem 7.\nL228: \nL229: Let $\\mathbf{K}$ be the NTK induced by the trainable parameters in $\\bm{\\theta}$, then if $\\kappa(\\mathbf{K}+\\sigma\\mathbf{I})\\leq c$, we have\nL230:  | $\\displaystyle\\frac{\\lambda_{\\text{max}}(\\mathbf{K}+\\mathbf{S}+\\sigma\\mathbf{I})}{a\\lambda_{\\text{max}}(\\mathbf{K}+\\sigma\\mathbf{I})}\\leq\\left(\\frac{\\mathcal{R}(\\bm{\\theta}\\cup\\hat{\\bm{\\theta}})}{\\mathcal{R}(\\bm{\\theta})}\\right)^{\\frac{1}{2}}\\leq\\frac{a\\lambda_{\\text{max}}(\\mathbf{K}+\\mathbf{S}+\\sigma\\mathbf{I})}{\\lambda_{\\text{max}}(\\mathbf{K}+\\sigma\\mathbf{I})},$  |  | (21)\nL231: where $a=\\frac{c}{(1-\\eta)^{2}}$, $\\eta=\\|\\mathbf{K}^{-1/2}\\mathbf{S}\\mathbf{K}^{-1/2}\\|$ and $\\mathbf{S}$ is the kernel induced by $\\hat{\\bm{\\theta}}$ with $[\\mathbf{S}]_{i,j}=\\nabla_{\\hat{\\bm{\\theta}}}f_{\\bm{\\theta}}\\left(\\mathbf{x}_{i}\\right)\\nabla_{\\hat{\\bm{\\theta}}}f_{\\bm{\\theta}}(\\mathbf{x}_{j})^{\\top}$.\nL232: ###### Proof.\nL233: \nL234: See Appendix cite24†H . ∎\nL235: \nL236: The above result implies that, by incorporating an additional layer to the parameters being fine-tuned, one can expect $\\frac{1}{2}\\log{\\frac{\\mathcal{R}(\\bm{\\theta}\\cup\\hat{\\bm{\\theta}})}{\\mathcal{R}(\\bm{\\theta})}}\\in\\left[\\log{\\frac{\\lambda_{\\text{max}}(\\mathbf{K}+\\mathbf{S})+\\sigma}{\\lambda_{\\text{max}}(\\mathbf{K})+\\sigma}}-\\log{a},\\log{\\frac{\\lambda_{\\text{max}}(\\mathbf{K}+\\mathbf{S})+\\sigma}{\\lambda_{\\text{max}}(\\mathbf{K})+\\sigma}}+\\log{a}\\right]$.\nL237: ###### Corollary 1.\nL238: \nL239: When we have two candidate layers $\\hat{\\bm{\\theta}}^{1}$ and $\\hat{\\bm{\\theta}}^{2}$, mutatis mutandis, we have\nL240:  | $\\displaystyle\\frac{\\lambda_{\\text{max}}(\\mathbf{K}+\\mathbf{S}_{1}+\\sigma\\mathbf{I})}{b\\lambda_{\\text{max}}(\\mathbf{K}+\\mathbf{S}_{2}+\\sigma\\mathbf{I})}$  | $\\displaystyle\\leq\\left(\\frac{\\mathcal{R}(\\bm{\\theta}\\cup\\hat{\\bm{\\theta}}^{1})}{\\mathcal{R}(\\bm{\\theta}\\cup\\hat{\\bm{\\theta}}^{2})}\\right)^{\\frac{1}{2}}\\leq\\frac{b\\lambda_{\\text{max}}(\\mathbf{K}+\\mathbf{S}_{1}+\\sigma\\mathbf{I})}{\\lambda_{\\text{max}}(\\mathbf{K}+\\mathbf{S}_{2}+\\sigma\\mathbf{I})},$  |  | (22)\nL241: where $b=a_{1}a_{2}$, $a_{{}_{l}}=\\frac{c}{(1-\\eta_{{}_{l}})^{2}}$, $\\eta_{{}_{l}}=\\|\\mathbf{K}^{-1/2}\\mathbf{S}_{{}_{l}}\\;\\mathbf{K}^{-1/2}\\|$ for $l\\in\\{1,2\\}$, and $\\mathbf{S}_{l}$ is the kernel induced by $\\hat{\\bm{\\theta}}^{l}$ with $[\\mathbf{S}_{l}]_{i,j}=\\nabla_{\\hat{\\bm{\\theta}}^{l}}f_{\\bm{\\theta}}\\left(\\mathbf{x}_{i}\\right)\\nabla_{\\hat{\\bm{\\theta}}^{l}}f_{\\bm{\\theta}}(\\mathbf{x}_{j})^{\\top}$.\nL242: As such, one can evaluate the candidacy of layers for fine-tuning based on Corollary cite69†1 .\nL243: In order to anticipate the empirical risk of fine-tuning, at initialization we look at a confined interval, i.e., $\\frac{1}{2}\\log{\\frac{\\mathcal{R}(\\bm{\\theta}\\cup\\hat{\\bm{\\theta}}^{1})}{\\mathcal{R}(\\bm{\\theta}\\cup\\hat{\\bm{\\theta}}^{2})}}\\in\\left[\\log{\\frac{\\lambda_{\\text{max}}(\\mathbf{K}+\\mathbf{S}_{1})+\\sigma}{\\lambda_{\\text{max}}(\\mathbf{K}+\\mathbf{S}_{2})+\\sigma}}-\\log{b},\\log{\\frac{\\lambda_{\\text{max}}(\\mathbf{K}+\\mathbf{S}_{1})+\\sigma}{\\lambda_{\\text{max}}(\\mathbf{K}+\\mathbf{S}_{2})+\\sigma}}+\\log{b}\\right]$.\nL244: ## 6 Experiments\nL245: Dataset  | Hyper-Parameter $\\lambda$  | 50  | 10  | 5  | 2  | 1  | 0.5  | 0.1  | 0.0\nL246: --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL247: CoLA  | $\\|\\bm{\\theta}_{t}-\\bm{\\theta}_{0}\\|_{2}$  | 0.280  | 0.350  | 0.404  | 0.5263  | 0.6148  | 0.6946  | 0.8223  | 0.960\nL248: $\\|f_{\\bm{\\theta}_{t}}(\\mathbf{X})-\\bar{f}_{\\bar{\\bm{\\theta}_{t}}}(\\mathbf{X})\\|_{2}$  | 1.06  | 1.12  | 1.39  | 1.25  | 1.27  | 1.32  | 1.28  | 1.47\nL249: KL Divergence  | 0.1060  | 0.1377  | 0.200  | 0.1613  | 0.1788  | 0.1961  | 0.1599  | 0.210\nL250: Evaluation Accuracy of $f_{\\bm{\\theta}_{t}}(\\mathbf{x})$  | 74.59  | 79.57  | 80.44  | 79.38  | 80.24  | 80.15  | 80.15  | 79.67\nL251: SST-2  | $\\|\\bm{\\theta}_{t}-\\bm{\\theta}_{0}\\|_{2}$  | 0.292  | 0.336  | 0.369  | 0.424  | 0.520  | 0.700  | 1.589  | 2.519\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA Statistical Framework for Alignment with Biased AI Feedback (https://arxiv.org/html/2602.08259v1)\nciteturn31650view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn31639view3\",\"lineno\":195}); Total lines: 458\nL163: This expression represents the bias as a weighted expectation of $\\widehat{Z}-Z$, where the weight adjusts for the distributional mismatch between the human- and AI-labeled datasets. Because $Y^{(1)}$ and $Y^{(2)}$ are independently sampled from the generation distribution $\\pi_{\\text{Gen}}^{\\text{Hum}}$, the bias expression is invariant to the ordering of the response pair. By symmetrizing over $(Y^{(1)},Y^{(2)})$, we obtain the following bias estimator based on the dataset $\\mathcal{D}_{\\text{Human}}$:\nL164:  | $$\\begin{split}\\widehat{\\mathrm{Bias}}(\\pi):=&\\frac{1}{2n}\\sum_{i=1}^{n}(\\widehat{Z}_{i}-Z_{i})\\frac{\\pi(Y_{i}^{(1)}\\mid X_{i})}{\\pi_{\\text{Gen}}^{\\text{Hum}}(Y_{i}^{(1)}\\mid X_{i})}\\frac{\\pi_{\\text{ref}}(Y_{i}^{(2)}\\mid X_{i})}{\\pi_{\\text{Gen}}^{\\text{Hum}}(Y_{i}^{(2)}\\mid X_{i})}\\\\\nL165: &+\\frac{1}{2n}\\sum_{i=1}^{n}(Z_{i}-\\widehat{Z}_{i})\\frac{\\pi(Y_{i}^{(2)}\\mid X_{i})}{\\pi_{\\text{Gen}}^{\\text{Hum}}(Y_{i}^{(2)}\\mid X_{i})}\\frac{\\pi_{\\text{ref}}(Y_{i}^{(1)}\\mid X_{i})}{\\pi_{\\text{Gen}}^{\\text{Hum}}(Y_{i}^{(1)}\\mid X_{i})},\\end{split}$$  |\nL166: where $\\widehat{Z}_{i}$ is generated from $(X_{i},Y_{i}^{(1)},Y_{i}^{(2)})$ by the AI evaluator. We define a debiased estimator of the IPO objective by subtracting the estimated bias,\nL167: \nL168:  | $$\\widehat{P}_{\\text{DIPO}}(\\pi):=\\widehat{P}_{\\text{DM}}(\\pi)-\\widehat{\\text{Bias}}(\\pi).$$  |  | (3.7)\nL169: \nL170: The corresponding debiased policy is obtained by maximizing the corrected objective subject to a KL regularization constraint,\nL171:  | $$\\widehat{\\pi}_{\\text{DIPO}}:=\\arg\\max_{\\pi\\in\\Pi}\\Big\\{\\widehat{P}_{\\text{DIPO}}(\\pi)-\\beta D_{\\text{KL}}\\big(\\pi\\|\\pi_{\\text{ref}}\\big)\\Big\\},$$  |  | (3.8)\nL172: \nL173: By incorporating the bias-corrected preference estimator $\\widehat{P}_{\\mathrm{DIPO}}(\\pi)$, the resulting policy $\\widehat{\\pi}_{\\mathrm{DIPO}}$ more closely reflects true human judgments while retaining the stability and regularization properties of IPO. The complete procedure is summarized in Algorithm cite48†2 .\nL174: Algorithm 2 Debiased Identity Preference Optimization (DIPO)\nL175: \nL176: 1: Reference policy $\\pi_{\\text{ref}}$, AI evaluation model, human-labeled dataset $\\mathcal{D}_{\\text{Human}}=\\{(X_{i},Y^{(1)}_{i},Y^{(2)}_{i},Z_{i},\\widehat{Z}_{i})\\}_{i=1}^{n}$, KL weight $\\beta$, clipping range $[c_{\\min},c_{\\max}]$, total steps $T$.\nL177: \nL178: 2: Initialize policy $\\pi^{(0)}\\leftarrow\\pi_{\\text{ref}}$.\nL179: \nL180: 3: for $t=1,\\dots,T$ do\nL181: \nL182: 4:   Sample a set of prompts $\\{X_{i}\\}_{i=n+1}^{n+N}$ from the prompt distribution $\\mathcal{D}_{X}$.\nL183: 5:   Sampling: $\\{Y_{i}\\sim\\pi^{(t-1)}(\\cdot\\mid X_{i})\\}_{i=n+1}^{n+N}$ and $\\{(Y_{i}^{(1)},Y_{i}^{(2)})\\sim\\pi_{\\text{ref}}(\\cdot\\mid X_{i})\\}_{i=n+1}^{n+N}$.\nL184: \nL185: 6:   Compute the direct-model preference estimate, $\\widehat{P}_{\\mathrm{DM}}(\\pi^{(t-1)})$, as defined in (cite49†3.6 ).\nL186: 7:   Compute importance ratios with clipping\nL187:  | $$w^{(1)}_{i}=\\text{clip}\\!\\left(\\frac{\\pi^{(t-1)}(Y_{i}^{(1)}\\mid X_{i})}{\\pi_{\\text{Gen}}^{\\text{Hum}}(Y_{i}^{(1)}\\mid X_{i})}\\frac{\\pi_{\\text{ref}}(Y_{i}^{(2)}\\mid X_{i})}{\\pi_{\\text{Gen}}^{\\text{Hum}}(Y_{i}^{(2)}\\mid X_{i})},\\,c_{\\min},\\,c_{\\max}\\right),$$  |\nL188:  | $$w^{(2)}_{i}=\\text{clip}\\!\\left(\\frac{\\pi^{(t-1)}(Y_{i}^{(2)}\\mid X_{i})}{\\pi_{\\text{Gen}}^{\\text{Hum}}(Y_{i}^{(2)}\\mid X_{i})}\\frac{\\pi_{\\text{ref}}(Y_{i}^{(1)}\\mid X_{i})}{\\pi_{\\text{Gen}}^{\\text{Hum}}(Y_{i}^{(1)}\\mid X_{i})},\\,c_{\\min},\\,c_{\\max}\\right).$$  |\nL189:  | $$\\widehat{\\mathrm{Bias}}^{(t-1)}\\;=\\;\\frac{1}{2n}\\sum_{i=1}^{n}(\\widehat{Z}_{i}-Z_{i})\\,w^{(1)}_{i}\\;+\\;\\frac{1}{2n}\\sum_{i=1}^{n}(Z_{i}-\\widehat{Z}_{i})\\,w^{(2)}_{i}.$$  |\nL190: 8:   Define the debiased IPO estimate at iteration $t$ as $\\widehat{P}_{\\mathrm{DIPO}}(\\pi^{(t-1)})$, following (cite50†3.7 ).\nL191: \nL192: 9:   Update $\\pi^{(t-1)}$ by $\\pi^{(t)}\\leftarrow\\pi^{(t-1)}+\\nabla_{\\pi}\\Big\\{\\widehat{P}_{\\mathrm{DIPO}}(\\pi^{(t-1)})-\\beta D_{\\text{KL}}\\big(\\pi^{(t-1)}\\|\\pi_{\\text{ref}}\\big)\\Big\\}.$\nL193: \nL194: 10: end for\nL195: \nL196: 11: return Debiased policy $\\widehat{\\pi}_{\\text{DIPO}}:=\\pi^{(T)}$.\nL197: ## 4 Theoretical Properties\nL198: \nL199: ### 4.1 Theoretical Results for DDPO\nL200: \nL201: We begin by stating a set of regularity conditions that are standard in the analysis of preference-based policy optimization.\nL202: \nL203: ###### Assumption 1 (Reward realizability).\nL204: \nL205: Let $\\Pi$ denote the policy class. The oracle reward function $r^{*}(\\cdot,\\cdot)$ belongs to the bounded reward class\nL206: \nL207:  | $$\\mathcal{R}=\\Bigl\\{\\beta\\log\\!\\frac{\\pi(Y\\mid X)}{\\pi_{\\mathrm{ref}}(Y\\mid X)}+\\beta W(X):\\;\\pi\\in\\Pi\\Bigr\\}.$$  |\nL208: ###### Assumption 2 (Boundedness).\nL209: \nL210: The reference policy $\\pi_{\\mathrm{ref}}(Y\\mid X)$ and the density ratios $w(Y^{(1)},Y^{(2)}\\mid X)$ are uniformly bounded away from zero by a constant $\\epsilon>0$.\nL211: \nL212: ###### Assumption 3 (Model complexity).\nL213: \nL214: The policy class $\\Pi$ is a Vapnik–Chervonenkis (VC) type class with finite VC index $v$ (Section 2.6 of cite51†van der Vaart and Wellner (2013) ).\nL215: ###### Assumption 4 (Alignment between AI and human preferences).\nL216: \nL217: The AI evaluation model and human preferences are aligned in the sense that\nL218: \nL219:  | $$\\mathbb{P}(Z_{i}\\neq\\widehat{Z}_{i}\\mid X,Y^{(1)},Y^{(2)})=O_{p}(\\nu_{n}).$$  |\nL220: Assumption cite52†1 ensures that the oracle reward function can be expressed as a log-ratio between the optimal policy and the reference policy, up to a normalization term depending on $X$. This representation is central to the equivalence between PPO and DPO under the BT model, allowing the DPO estimator to recover the optimal policy at the population level (cite28†Rafailov et al., 2023 ).\nL221: The boundedness condition in Assumption cite53†2 guarantees that likelihood ratios and importance weights remain well behaved, ruling out degenerate cases in which relevant responses receive vanishing probability. Such overlap-type conditions are standard in causal inference (cite54†Imbens and Rubin, 2015 ) and off-policy evaluation (cite55†Uehara et al., 2022 ; cite56†Zhan et al., 2024 ). Assumption cite57†3 controls the richness of the policy class.\nL222: The VC index $v$ governs the uniform convergence rate of empirical objectives and appears explicitly in the suboptimality bounds derived below. Assumption cite58†4 quantifies the degree of agreement between AI-generated preference labels and true human judgments. The parameter $\\nu_{n}$ measures the probability of disagreement between the two mechanisms. Smaller values of $\\nu_{n}$ correspond to closer alignment, in which case fewer human-labeled samples are sufficient to correct the bias.\nL223: This assumption is mild in practice when the AI evaluator has been trained or fine-tuned using high-quality human preference data.\nL224: ###### Theorem 1 (Suboptimality gap for DDPO).\nL225: \nL226: Under Assumptions cite52†1 –cite58†4 , the suboptimality gap of the DDPO estimator satisfies\nL227:  | $$\\begin{split}&\\mathbb{E}_{X\\sim\\mathcal{D}_{X}}\\Big[\\mathbb{E}_{Y\\sim\\pi^{*}(\\cdot\\mid X)}r(X,Y)-\\mathbb{E}_{Y\\sim\\widehat{\\pi}_{\\mathrm{DDPO}}(\\cdot\\mid X)}r(X,Y)\\Big]\\\\\nL228: &\\quad=O\\!\\left(\\frac{1}{\\beta\\,\\epsilon}\\sqrt{\\frac{v}{N}}+\\frac{1}{\\beta\\,\\epsilon}\\sqrt{\\frac{v\\nu_{n}}{n}}+\\frac{1}{\\beta\\,\\epsilon}\\nu_{n}^{1/2}\\,\\|\\widehat{w}-w\\|_{2}\\right),\\end{split}$$  |  | (4.1)\nL229: \nL230: where $\\pi^{*}$ denotes the oracle optimal policy defined in (cite59†3.1 ) and\nL231:  | $$\\|\\widehat{w}-w\\|_{2}:=\\Bigg\\{\\mathbb{E}_{X\\sim\\mathcal{D}_{X},\\,(Y^{(1)},Y^{(2)})\\sim\\pi_{\\mathrm{Gen}}^{\\mathrm{Hum}}(\\cdot\\mid X)}\\big[w(Y^{(1)},Y^{(2)}\\mid X)-\\widehat{w}(Y^{(1)},Y^{(2)}\\mid X)\\big]^{2}\\Bigg\\}^{1/2}.$$  |\nL232: Theorem cite60†1 provides a finite-sample upper bound on the performance loss of the DDPO policy relative to the oracle optimum. The first term reflects estimation error from the AI-labeled dataset of size $N$, and the second term captures the contribution of the human-labeled dataset of size $n$, scaled by the disagreement rate $\\nu_{n}$. When $\\nu_{n}$ is small, only a limited number of human-labeled comparisons is required to achieve consistent debiasing.\nL233: The third term quantifies the impact of density-ratio estimation. It vanishes when the human data are generated under the same policy as the AI-labeled data or when the generation mechanism is known. This term is analogous to the cross-term appearing in double machine learning estimators (cite61†Chernozhukov et al., 2018 ).\nL234: In many practical settings, the AI-labeled dataset is substantially larger than the human-labeled dataset, while the disagreement rate $\\nu_{n}$ is small but non-negligible. In this regime, the debiased estimator substantially reduces the bias inherited from the AI evaluation model. From another perspective, accurate AI evaluation enhances the effective sample size of human-labeled data, reducing the convergence rate from $\\sqrt{v/n}$ to $\\sqrt{v\\nu_{n}/n}$ and thereby improving sample efficiency.\nL235: The bound in Theorem cite60†1 also highlights the role of the regularization parameter $\\beta$. Minimal values of $\\beta$ are undesirable, as the KL regularization becomes weak and the objective can exhibit numerical instability. Since DDPO updates scale inversely with $\\beta$, small reward differences may be excessively amplified, leading to unstable policy updates. This behavior is consistent with empirical findings in cite62†Wu et al.\nL236: (2024) and recent theoretical analyses of DPO-type objectives (cite63†Xu et al., 2025 ).\nL237: ### 4.2 Theoretical Results for DIPO\nL238: \nL239: We begin by characterizing the DIPO estimator through an influence function expansion (cite64†van der Vaart, 2000 ), which clarifies how bias correction and variance reduction arise from combining AI-labeled and human-labeled preference data. We first consider the estimation of the preference probability $\\mathbb{P}(\\pi(\\cdot)\\succ\\pi_{\\mathrm{ref}}(\\cdot))$ using only human-labeled data.\nL240: In this setting, to achieve statistically optimal estimation of the preference probability, we adopt a semiparametrically efficient estimator. The estimator is obtained as the solution to the estimating equation\nL241: \nL242:  | $$\\mathbb{E}\\!\\left[\\psi(X,Y^{(1)},Y^{(2)},Z;\\pi,\\pi_{\\mathrm{ref}},\\pi_{\\mathrm{Gen}}^{\\mathrm{Hum}},g)\\right]=0,$$  |\nL243: \nL244: where the influence function $\\psi(\\cdot)$ is given by\nL245:  | $$\\begin{split}&\\mathbb{E}_{Y\\sim\\pi(\\cdot\\mid X),\\,Y^{\\prime}\\sim\\pi_{\\mathrm{ref}}(\\cdot\\mid X)}\\big\\{g(Y,Y^{\\prime}\\mid X)\\big\\}\\\\\nL246: &\\;+\\frac{1}{2}\\{Z-g(Y^{(1)},Y^{(2)}\\mid X)\\}\\frac{\\pi(Y^{(1)}\\mid X)\\pi_{\\mathrm{ref}}(Y^{(2)}\\mid X)-\\pi(Y^{(2)}\\mid X)\\pi_{\\mathrm{ref}}(Y^{(1)}\\mid X)}{\\pi_{\\mathrm{Gen}}^{\\mathrm{Hum}}(Y^{(1)}\\mid X)\\pi_{\\mathrm{Gen}}^{\\mathrm{Hum}}(Y^{(2)}\\mid X)}.\\end{split}$$  |  | (4.2)\nL247: The first term corresponds to a direct plug-in estimator of the preference probability under the human preference model $g$, while the second term augments this estimate with a weighted residual. This augmentation corrects for model misspecification and yields a doubly robust estimating equation: consistency holds if either $g$ or $\\pi_{\\mathrm{Gen}}^{\\mathrm{Hum}}$ is known or correctly specified.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNear-Oracle KV Selection via Pre-hoc Sparsity for Long-Context Inference (https://arxiv.org/html/2602.08329v1)\nciteturn31650view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2602.08329v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 994\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOJBKQ: Objective-Joint Babai-Klein Quantization (https://arxiv.org/html/2602.08376v1)\nciteturn31650view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2602.08376v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 516\n"
